<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#trierarchy">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trierarchy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as trierarchia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A trierarchy was a liturgy, a public service levied on the very wealthy of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, that made a citizen, the trierarch, responsible for outfitting, maintaining and leading a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">trireme</a> warship, usually for a year, while the state provided the ship, its equipment and its crew. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> dates it to at least the tyranny of Hippias (527–510 BCE), and Athens was levying it by 480 BCE. By 408 BCE the burden could be shared in a syntrierarchy; Periandros's Law of 358/7 BCE spread it over 1,200 registered men in twenty divisions, and a law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a> in 340 BCE raised the register to 2,000. Trierarchs were still appointed as late as 224/3 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trierarchy.jpg" alt="Trierarchy" width="330" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tungsten · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greek_Galleys.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The exact origin of the trierarchy is disputed. Its growth followed the Athenian naval buildup of the early 5th century BCE, paid for by a rise in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from the mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laurion">Laurion</a> in 482 BCE. The great cost of the service probably explains the syntrierarchy, in which two or more men shared a ship and agreed its command between themselves privately; this also widened the pool of possible trierarchs. By the mid-350s BCE too few eligible citizens were willing to give their time and money, which prompted the reforms of Periandros and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a>. Under Periandros's Law the cost still fell equally on members of unequal wealth, and the full register was often not assembled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Only a wealthy, property-owning citizen could be named, often at the start of an expedition, and he served under the generals, the strategoi. The treasurer gave a Trierarchic Crown to the first trierarch to have his ship ready, and one who failed to appear by the last day of the set month could be arrested. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">trireme</a> carried about 200 men: the trierarch, 16 petty officers, 10 to 20 marines and the rowers. The officers included the helmsman, the boatswain, the bow officer, a shipwright and a piper who kept the rowers in time.</p><p>Building a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">trireme</a> cost about one talent, 26 kilograms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. Crews were usually conscripted from official lists and paid between two obols and a drachma a day, so a month at sea at a drachma a man cost another talent. A trierarch could add to the pay or hire seasoned men at his own expense, and his successor repaid him for any improvements left on the ship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamian-war">Lamian War</a> of 323 to 322 BCE the Athenian fleet shrank sharply, and the Macedonians held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a> from 294 to 224 BCE. The historian Christian Ammitzbøll Thomsen believes the number of trierarchs fell while their duties stayed largely the same. Elsewhere the word came to mean a ship's commander. In Lebedos and Teos the trierarchy was a liturgy, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, while in Ptolemaic Egypt trierarchs were paid directly from the royal treasury in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trierarchy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their wealth was expected to serve the city through the liturgies: a <b>trierarch</b> equipped and commanded a warship for a year, at a cost of several thousand drachmas, and a choregos paid for a chorus at the drama festivals.</blockquote><a href="/societies/athens">Social Hierarchy of Classical Athens</a></li></ul></section>
